      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14257592/assets/a031d56566fb7cabbb41591eb5a5be7bd54f98f2eeb25cfcf96190b7d3f481ad.png)\n\nThe graph above shows a section of the function $y=\\dfrac 1 2\\sec{(x+C)},$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\pi <                C <                \\pi,$ what is the value of $C?$",
      "worked_solution": "The constant $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function $y=\\dfrac 1 2\\sec x.$\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the given curve is a secant graph that's been shifted **right** by $1$ unit. So, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac 1 2\\sec\\left(x-1\\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore, $C =-1.$",
